# RML mapping: counterfactual worlds JSON -> cw: RDF (Plan 2 Layers B and C).
#
# The companion of `ocg_mapping.rml.ttl`, and subject to exactly the same
# SDM-RDFizer 4.7.5 constraints — read that file's header first; the reasoning
# behind the denormalised source, the literal IRI stems in subject templates,
# the `rr:template` + `rr:termType rr:IRI` idiom for foreign IRIs, and the
# refusal to put a quote in a literal is documented there and is not repeated.
#
# What this maps is one *hypothetical world*: for a single unit, under a joint
# intervention over one or more causal variables, what every other node's value
# would most likely have been.  Five decisions shape it.
#
#   1. **The query IRI is the world.**  Plan 2 §6.0 principle 1: a world is
#      identified by the query that defines it, so there is no separate world
#      resource with nothing of its own to say.  The set of `cw:hasIntervention`
#      hanging off one `cw:CounterfactualQuery` *is* the joint intervention.
#
#   2. **One estimate per non-intervened node.**  A world is only interesting
#      because it says what else would have been different, so there is one
#      `cw:CounterfactualEstimate` per node rather than one for a nominated
#      target.  Intervened nodes are excluded: their value is the treatment,
#      already stated once as `cw:setValue`, and repeating it as a prediction
#      would dress an assumption up as a result.
#
#   3. **`cw:factualValue` sits beside `cw:predictedValue`.**  A counterfactual
#      without its baseline is unreadable: "dosage would have been 40" means
#      nothing until "dosage was 25" is beside it.  The contrast between them is
#      deliberately *not* minted as a term — it is a subtraction.
#
#   4. **No distribution sub-tree.**  An earlier version wrote a
#      `cw:CategoricalDistribution` with one `cw:Outcome` child per level:
#      `1 + k` extra subjects per node per world, dominating the document to
#      answer a question nobody asked.  What survives is one triple,
#      `cw:probability`, carrying the mass of the arg-max level that
#      `cw:predictedValue` reports.  Continuous nodes get `cw:standardDeviation`
#      instead — read only together with the world's `cw:rowCount`, which says
#      whether flat-join row duplication is folded into that spread.
#
#   5. **`entity cw:hasQuery world` is written when there is an entity.**  This
#      is the §6.1 annotation form: it adds no `entity property value` triple, so
#      merging this document into the source KG contradicts nothing in it, while
#      still letting a consumer start from a patient and find every hypothetical
#      asked about them.  A *hypothetical* unit — observed values supplied by
#      hand, no resource behind them — simply carries no `entity` key, and both
#      `cw:aboutEntity` and this map fall silent for it.  Projecting the
#      hypothetical values as real triples needs `GRAPH <world> { ... }` (§6.8)
#      and therefore a quad format; Turtle cannot express it and this mapping
#      does not attempt it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why there are three arrays for every value, instead of one
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A causal value is a literal, a *typed* literal, or an IRI, and the engine can
# switch between those only per triples map — never per row:
#
#   * there is no `rml:termTypeMap`, so IRI-valued rows (an object-typed causal
#     variable, whose value is a resource of the source KG) cannot share a
#     predicate-object map with literal ones;
#   * `rml:datatypeMap` *is* supported — `rml:datatypeMap [ rml:template "{f}" ]`
#     types a literal per row, which is what makes `"111.017"^^xsd:double`
#     possible at all — but it is unforgiving: a row whose datatype field is
#     **empty** has that value silently dropped, and a row where the field is
#     **absent entirely** takes *the whole triples map* down with it. Verified
#     against 4.7.5.
#
# Hence `estimates` / `estimates_typed` / `estimates_object` (and the same three
# for interventions).  `causalway.cf_export` guarantees every row of a `*_typed`
# array carries a `datatype`, and that a node's counterfactual and factual values
# share one datatype — they are the same variable, so typing one `xsd:integer`
# and the other `xsd:double` would claim they are different kinds of quantity.
#
# Note that an absent *ordinary* reference (`factual`, `probability`, `std`) is
# handled correctly: no triple, map unaffected.  Only the datatype field is
# fatal.
#
# Run diagnostics (sample counts, effective sample size, the JSON blob under
# `cw:diagnostics`) are **not** exported. §6.6 keeps only `cw:computedAt`, and
# a JSON literal could not survive the engine's quote rewriting anyway. The
# noise coupling and `cw:randomSeed` *are* kept, because they are what make the
# number reproducible rather than merely explained.

@prefix rr:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/r2rml#> .
@prefix rml:  <http://semweb.mmlab.be/ns/rml#> .
@prefix ql:   <http://semweb.mmlab.be/ns/ql#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix cw:  <http://sdm-causalway.org/> .


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ #
# The world == the counterfactual query
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ #
<#WorldMap>
    a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource [
        rml:source "cf.json" ;
        rml:referenceFormulation ql:JSONPath ;
        rml:iterator "$.worlds[*]"
    ] ;
    rr:subjectMap [
        rr:template "http://sdm-causalway.org/query/{world_key}" ;
        rr:class cw:CounterfactualQuery
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate rdfs:label ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "label" ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:aboutEntity ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{entity}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:usedModel ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{model_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:computedAt ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "computed_at" ; rr:datatype xsd:dateTime ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:counterfactualCoupling ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "coupling" ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:rowCount ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "row_count" ; rr:datatype xsd:integer ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:randomSeed ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "random_seed" ; rr:datatype xsd:integer ]
    ] .

# Entity-first navigation (§6.1): from a patient to every world asked about them.
#
# This is the one triples map whose *subject* is a foreign IRI, and the idiom is
# the exact inverse of the object-map one: a subject `rr:template "{entity}"`
# percent-encodes and re-bases (giving
# `http://example.com/base/http%3A%2F%2F...`), while `rml:reference` +
# `rr:termType rr:IRI` passes it through verbatim. In an *object* map the two
# swap roles. Verified against SDM-RDFizer 4.7.5; do not "make them consistent".
#
# This map reads `worlds_named`, not `worlds`, and that is not tidiness. A
# missing key in *object* position yields no triple, as documented in
# `ocg_mapping.rml.ttl`; in *subject* position under `rml:reference` it crashes
# the engine — `subject_value[1:-1]` on a None, with none of the `try/except`
# that guards every template branch beside it (4.7.5, `__init__.py:4809`).
# A hypothetical world has no entity, so `cf_export` filters those rows out in
# Python and this map never sees one.
<#EntityHasQueryMap>
    a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource [
        rml:source "cf.json" ;
        rml:referenceFormulation ql:JSONPath ;
        rml:iterator "$.worlds_named[*]"
    ] ;
    rr:subjectMap [ rml:reference "entity" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:hasQuery ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{world_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] .


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ #
# The acts that define the world.  Intervention IRIs are minted from their
# content (Plan 2 §6.3), so two worlds applying the same do() to the same
# unit share one resource rather than duplicating it.
#
# Three maps, one per value form; everything but `cw:setValue` is identical
# between them, and that repetition is the price of the engine's per-map
# term-type and datatype binding.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ #
<#InterventionMap>
    a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource [
        rml:source "cf.json" ;
        rml:referenceFormulation ql:JSONPath ;
        rml:iterator "$.interventions[*]"
    ] ;
    rr:subjectMap [
        rr:template "http://sdm-causalway.org/intervention/{intervention_key}" ;
        rr:class cw:Intervention
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onNode ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{node_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onProperty ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{property}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onEntity ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{entity}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:setValue ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "value" ]
    ] .

<#InterventionTypedMap>
    a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource [
        rml:source "cf.json" ;
        rml:referenceFormulation ql:JSONPath ;
        rml:iterator "$.interventions_typed[*]"
    ] ;
    rr:subjectMap [
        rr:template "http://sdm-causalway.org/intervention/{intervention_key}" ;
        rr:class cw:Intervention
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onNode ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{node_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onProperty ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{property}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onEntity ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{entity}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:setValue ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "value" ; rml:datatypeMap [ rml:template "{datatype}" ] ]
    ] .

# An object-typed causal variable: the hypothetical treatment is a *resource*
# of the source KG, not a label for one.
<#InterventionObjectMap>
    a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource [
        rml:source "cf.json" ;
        rml:referenceFormulation ql:JSONPath ;
        rml:iterator "$.interventions_object[*]"
    ] ;
    rr:subjectMap [
        rr:template "http://sdm-causalway.org/intervention/{intervention_key}" ;
        rr:class cw:Intervention
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onNode ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{node_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onProperty ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{property}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onEntity ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{entity}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:setValue ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{value_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] .

# The world -> intervention link, once per array (the link itself is the same
# triple whatever form the value took).
<#WorldHasInterventionMap>
    a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource [
        rml:source "cf.json" ;
        rml:referenceFormulation ql:JSONPath ;
        rml:iterator "$.interventions[*]"
    ] ;
    rr:subjectMap [ rr:template "http://sdm-causalway.org/query/{world_key}" ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:hasIntervention ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{intervention_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] .

<#WorldHasInterventionTypedMap>
    a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource [
        rml:source "cf.json" ;
        rml:referenceFormulation ql:JSONPath ;
        rml:iterator "$.interventions_typed[*]"
    ] ;
    rr:subjectMap [ rr:template "http://sdm-causalway.org/query/{world_key}" ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:hasIntervention ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{intervention_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] .

<#WorldHasInterventionObjectMap>
    a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource [
        rml:source "cf.json" ;
        rml:referenceFormulation ql:JSONPath ;
        rml:iterator "$.interventions_object[*]"
    ] ;
    rr:subjectMap [ rr:template "http://sdm-causalway.org/query/{world_key}" ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:hasIntervention ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{intervention_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] .


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ #
# The answer: one estimate per non-intervened node, carrying what the node
# actually was, what it would most likely have been, and how confident that is.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ #
<#EstimateMap>
    a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource [
        rml:source "cf.json" ;
        rml:referenceFormulation ql:JSONPath ;
        rml:iterator "$.estimates[*]"
    ] ;
    rr:subjectMap [
        rr:template "http://sdm-causalway.org/answer/{world_key}/{node_key}" ;
        rr:class cw:CounterfactualEstimate
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:forQuery ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{world_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onNode ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{node_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onProperty ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{property}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onEntity ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{entity}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:predictedValue ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "value" ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:factualValue ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "factual" ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:probability ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "probability" ; rr:datatype xsd:double ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:standardDeviation ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "std" ; rr:datatype xsd:double ]
    ] .

<#EstimateTypedMap>
    a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource [
        rml:source "cf.json" ;
        rml:referenceFormulation ql:JSONPath ;
        rml:iterator "$.estimates_typed[*]"
    ] ;
    rr:subjectMap [
        rr:template "http://sdm-causalway.org/answer/{world_key}/{node_key}" ;
        rr:class cw:CounterfactualEstimate
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:forQuery ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{world_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onNode ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{node_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onProperty ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{property}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onEntity ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{entity}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:predictedValue ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "value" ; rml:datatypeMap [ rml:template "{datatype}" ] ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:factualValue ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "factual" ; rml:datatypeMap [ rml:template "{datatype}" ] ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:probability ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "probability" ; rr:datatype xsd:double ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:standardDeviation ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "std" ; rr:datatype xsd:double ]
    ] .

# An object-typed causal variable: both values are resources of the source KG.
# Such a node is categorical by construction, so it carries `cw:probability`
# and never `cw:standardDeviation`.
<#EstimateObjectMap>
    a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource [
        rml:source "cf.json" ;
        rml:referenceFormulation ql:JSONPath ;
        rml:iterator "$.estimates_object[*]"
    ] ;
    rr:subjectMap [
        rr:template "http://sdm-causalway.org/answer/{world_key}/{node_key}" ;
        rr:class cw:CounterfactualEstimate
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:forQuery ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{world_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onNode ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{node_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onProperty ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{property}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:onEntity ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{entity}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:predictedValue ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{value_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:factualValue ;
        rr:objectMap [ rr:template "{factual_iri}" ; rr:termType rr:IRI ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate cw:probability ;
        rr:objectMap [ rml:reference "probability" ; rr:datatype xsd:double ]
    ] .
